{"id":"1c5d29b9-f067-4353-ba61-d36f59584c69","object":"chat.completion","created":1784835705,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be dict\\\")\\n    if 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"missing threshold or calls\\\")\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"threshold must be number\\\")\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be list\\\")\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    accepted = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each call must be dict\\\")\\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\\n        for key in required:\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"missing {key}\\\")\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"loss_without_call must be number\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"loss_with_call must be number\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(tokens, int) or tokens < 1:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive int\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"parseable must be bool\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        norm_gain = gain / tokens\\n        diag = {\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\n            'gain': round(gain, 6),\\n            'normalized_gain': round(norm_gain, 6)\\n        }\\n        diagnostics.append(diag)\\n        if parseable and norm_gain >= threshold:\\n            accepted.append(call_id)\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code for function `solve(case)`. The task: filter API calls based on losses, normalized gain, threshold, parseable. Compute per call: gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call, normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens (or maybe at least one? Actually \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" Means if prediction_tokens is 0? But given data, tokens positive. Use max(tokens,1) perhaps. But field is prediction_tokens, integer, presumably >=1. So just divide. Then filter: parseable == True and normalized_gain >= threshold. Also \"stronger_counterfactual\" maybe refers to using loss_without_call vs loss_with_call? Already subtracted. Return accepted call IDs list, and per-call diagnostics: normalized_gain (rounded?), maybe also raw gain? The spec: \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" What diagnostics? Possibly include normalized_gain, gain? The objective says \"normalized_weights\" - but we don't have weights. Also \"inclusive_threshold\" means >= threshold. \"stable_order\" means keep order. Also \"aggregate values\" maybe not needed? The output should be a JSON-serializable object. Looking at typical such problems, the output might be a dict with keys: \"accepted_call_ids\" (list of strings), \"diagnostics\" (list of dicts with call_id, normalized_gain, maybe gain?). But we need exact named fields. The problem says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" No further specification. I'll assume standard format: {\"accepted_call_ids\": [...], \"diagnostics\": [{\"call_id\": ..., \"normalized_gain\": ..., \"gain\": ...} for each call in order? Or only accepted? The phrase \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" suggests for each call? But \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" Could be all calls. However the problem says \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Likely diagnostics for each accepted call? But then \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order\" applies to the full list. I think we should return diagnostics for all calls in original order, not just accepted. But then why mention \"accepted call IDs\"? Possibly return both lists. To be safe, I'll output a dict with \"accepted_call_ids\" and \"diagnostics\" (list of dicts with call_id, normalized_gain, gain). For diagnostics, include all calls in original order. That matches \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" Also \"Compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions\" - maybe not needed.\n\nCheck for invalid case: Raise ValueError if missing fields or type errors.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate case has 'threshold' and 'calls' (list). Each call has call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. All numeric, parseable bool.\n2. Compute gain = loss_without - loss_with\n3. normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens (assuming positive)\n4. Determine accepted: parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n5. Build diagnostics list: for each call in order, dict with call_id, normalized_gain (rounded to something? Not specified. Probably round to 8 decimal places like data? Use round(gain,8) for diagnostics? But spec says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". I'll round to 6 decimal places? Or just keep as float? Better to use round(value, 8) to match precision. But the objective says \"rounded\" - maybe to 2 decimals? Unclear. I'll round to 6 decimals to be safe, but not required. However the examples don't show output. The fixture only shows input. We'll just keep as float without rounding, or round to 8. I'll use round(value, 6) to avoid floating point noise. But the test might expect exact values from division. Since we only use arithmetic, we can round to 6 decimal places. I'll round gain and normalized_gain to 6 decimals. The output must be JSON-serializable, float is fine.\n6. Return dict.\n\nAlso \"normalized_weights\" - not present. Ignore.\n\nWrite solve function.\n\nNow produce JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a JSON string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nMake sure to use only standard library.\n\nLet's craft code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate presence and types\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be dict\")\n    if 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"missing threshold or calls\")\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be number\")\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be list\")\n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted = []\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be dict\")\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\n        for key in required:\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"missing {key}\")\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_without_call must be number\")\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_with_call must be number\")\n        if not isinstance(tokens, int) or tokens < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be positive int\")\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"parseable must be bool\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        norm_gain = gain / tokens\n        # round diagnostics\n        diag = {\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'gain': round(gain, 6),\n            'normalized_gain': round(norm_gain, 6)\n        }\n        diagnostics.append(diag)\n        if parseable and norm_gain >= threshold:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nBut ensure we don't mutate case. We only read.\n\nNow produce JSON string. Need to escape backslashes, newlines, quotes. We'll write as a single line with \\n for newlines, and double quotes escaped as \\\". Also backslashes need to be escaped.\n\nI'll write the implementation string. Then embed in JSON.\n\nMake sure the outer JSON is valid: {\"implementation\": \"<escaped string>\"}\n\nLet's construct the string carefully."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1762,"completion_tokens":2024,"total_tokens":3786,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1495},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":98},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}